WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:16.680
Welcome back to the PolicyViz podcast. I'm your host, John Schwabisch. Now we're into 2018. It's

00:16.680 --> 00:22.080
probably a good time to look ahead, but also to look back a little bit on what happened last year

00:22.080 --> 00:26.640
with data visualization, what happened in the last several years, and of course, what's going

00:26.640 --> 00:32.400
to happen in 2018. And to help me do that, I'm very excited to have Ben Welsh, who is the editor

00:32.400 --> 00:37.240
of the data desk at the LA Times to chat about what's going on over there on the west coast.

00:37.240 --> 00:41.520
Ben, how are you? Happy New Year. Thanks for coming on the show. Happy New Year, John. Thanks

00:41.520 --> 00:46.760
for having me. I'm excited to chat about all things LA Times because some of the great stuff

00:46.760 --> 00:51.360
that you guys do that tends to be west coast doesn't always make it over here to the east

00:51.360 --> 00:54.800
coast. You know, it's sort of like the little story about the wildfires. And then I get to go

00:54.800 --> 01:01.120
to the LA Times website, and really see some incredible stuff about wildfires or agriculture

01:01.120 --> 01:05.480
work that doesn't sort of make the front page over here. So why don't we start with having you talk

01:05.480 --> 01:09.620
a little bit about yourself and how long you've been at the times and the work that you all do

01:09.620 --> 01:14.880
there, and how that data desk works in relation to the rest of the newsroom? Yeah, sure, no problem.

01:14.880 --> 01:23.760
So I've been doing the kind of nerdy thing for, you know, gosh, I guess I have to count now. I

01:24.240 --> 01:30.560
guess probably about 15 years. And about the last 10 of those have been at the LA Times. And when I

01:30.560 --> 01:36.120
started in 2007, I was working for la times.com, which was in an entirely different part of the

01:36.120 --> 01:43.120
building and wasn't even in the editorial quote unquote section of the newspaper. And in the 10

01:43.120 --> 01:47.360
years since that's changed. And we've kind of gradually integrated and we have one newsroom now.

01:47.360 --> 01:52.800
And within that one newsroom at actually kind of the center of it physically is where our team,

01:52.800 --> 01:59.040
the data desk sits. And we're kind of a small squad of reporters and computer programmers who

01:59.040 --> 02:05.600
use our technical skills to get stuff done, to find and tell stories to solve problems to do

02:05.600 --> 02:12.560
research to make things happen on the internet. And there's really a lot of different shapes and

02:12.560 --> 02:17.680
sizes that takes I don't think any of it would really surprise you. You know, one observation I

02:17.680 --> 02:22.560
would kind of make about our industry is that the types of things people are doing are really

02:22.640 --> 02:28.640
quite similar from place to place. But the way in which they're done sort of within the organization

02:28.640 --> 02:33.040
or who does it can vary quite a bit, you know, kind of the mix of different tasks that are done by

02:33.040 --> 02:38.480
the mix of different teams in newsrooms can vary a bit. And even though our team is called the data

02:38.480 --> 02:44.160
desk, we do a little more than that, just because of the way things have evolved. And so in addition

02:44.160 --> 02:49.040
to the kind of, you know, old school computer assisted reporting type stuff where you're

02:49.040 --> 02:56.160
generating nutgrass from big hairy databases. We also do a lot of systems administration for

02:56.160 --> 03:02.000
newsroom tools that help us publish custom stuff, and fancy stories and things on the web, we do

03:02.640 --> 03:07.040
the design for a lot of the long form and fancy packages that come through each year.

03:07.760 --> 03:12.800
And we do some data visualization and other work as well. And it really can kind of vary

03:12.800 --> 03:17.760
depending on the project and the people involved and what needs to get done. So I tend to describe

03:17.760 --> 03:22.480
our team as kind of the nerd Swiss army knife, where we're trying to use our computer skills to

03:22.480 --> 03:27.520
solve problems and make stuff happen. Right. I mean, I assume there's a big culture shift having,

03:27.520 --> 03:32.640
you know, move the two newsrooms together. What was the transition like from going from a newsroom

03:32.640 --> 03:39.520
that was your I would guess a traditional shoe leather newsroom and a separate sort of dot com

03:39.520 --> 03:45.040
newsroom and then pushing those together? Was there a big culture change, a big effort to try

03:45.040 --> 03:50.640
to merge those two different I would guess cultures and skill sets together? Yeah, I mean,

03:50.640 --> 03:54.480
so when I started the la times.com was in a different part of the building with a different

03:54.480 --> 03:59.040
reporting structure, I was hired there and didn't even work, you know, I guess for the editorial

03:59.040 --> 04:03.440
department. And now you know, I'm I guess an editor within it and we're all employees under

04:03.440 --> 04:07.840
that umbrella. So like organizationally, it was all kind of moved under, which was done

04:07.840 --> 04:14.480
in kind of a big reorganization that went along with some pretty large layoffs. A number of years

04:14.480 --> 04:20.720
ago, and that was kind of, you know, a big moment for the LA times when all that happened. But,

04:20.720 --> 04:27.200
you know, just one change like that doesn't sort everything out. These types of changes are really

04:27.200 --> 04:33.120
long and complicated and evolutionary and uneven depending on, you know, where you are or what

04:33.120 --> 04:39.360
you're doing. And it's kind of really a struggle that everyone in the industry is facing. And we

04:39.360 --> 04:44.960
are to just try to, you know, get to where we need to be. I don't think there was ever one moment

04:44.960 --> 04:50.000
where suddenly, you know, the switch went on and things were different. But there's been milestones

04:50.000 --> 04:54.240
throughout, you know, that was a big one when the merger happened, like another big one that's very

04:54.240 --> 04:59.760
procedural that I'm proud our team was involved in was having all of our copy being written in the

04:59.760 --> 05:05.520
web CMS first and then exported to a print publishing platform, rather than vice versa,

05:05.520 --> 05:10.240
which might not sound too oppressive for people who work on websites and haven't worked in a print

05:10.240 --> 05:14.960
newsroom. But the reality is is actually the reverse most places where you're writing in a print CMS

05:14.960 --> 05:20.400
and then at some boring bureaucratic process has to play out before it is, you know, exported to

05:20.400 --> 05:26.320
the website. And just even reversing that last year to me was in its own way kind of a major

05:26.320 --> 05:32.800
milestone in the LA Times history. Yeah, were there certain stories over that time span that

05:33.760 --> 05:38.560
maybe drew people in even more engaged them to say, Oh, this is something that I can use in my

05:38.560 --> 05:43.360
reporting to, you know, connect with or communicate better to the audience? Like, were there milestone

05:43.360 --> 05:47.920
projects that you remember over that time as well? Well, there's definitely been a lot of big projects

05:47.920 --> 05:51.600
that you know, I'm proud of on our team. I don't know if we can necessarily take credit to having,

05:51.600 --> 05:56.400
you know, done the big story that that changed the whole thing. I don't know. I don't know if you

05:56.400 --> 06:00.880
could say there's one story that really flip things here. But I would say, you know, there's

06:00.880 --> 06:05.680
projects we've been involved on that were that were entirely kind of untraditional, that didn't

06:05.680 --> 06:13.600
involve creating, like a traditional print story at all. Things like we had a project, you know,

06:13.600 --> 06:19.680
eight or nine years ago, when I first started called California's war dead, where we compiled

06:19.680 --> 06:24.400
all the obituaries that had been written about Californians who died or Iraq and Afghanistan,

06:24.400 --> 06:29.200
made it into a database, then made that database into a website that had a page for each person,

06:29.200 --> 06:34.640
and then also powered a lot of enterprise pieces. And then on a going forward basis, after that,

06:34.640 --> 06:39.760
we were publishing to that application before we were even researching and writing the obituaries

06:39.760 --> 06:44.560
and sort of incrementally updating them as the work continued. And I think that that because the

06:44.560 --> 06:49.600
obituary writing process was a project that so many people in the newsroom were involved in,

06:49.600 --> 06:55.280
in the writing and research for, I think it was a way for us to expose a lot of people to doing

06:55.280 --> 07:00.400
things kind of a different way, and kind of conceiving of what you're creating in a more

07:00.400 --> 07:05.200
structured but also, you know, less conventional storytelling format. And so I guess in my time

07:05.200 --> 07:10.080
here, I think that's been a big one. Yeah. Now that you have the newsrooms together, and we've

07:10.080 --> 07:14.640
seen a big change in technology and the way people are receiving their news, can you talk a little

07:14.640 --> 07:21.440
bit about the balance between desktop and mobile and how you all think about that as you're creating

07:21.440 --> 07:25.920
either the small projects or even these big narrative features? Sure. I mean, everything

07:25.920 --> 07:30.080
has got to work on the phone. If it doesn't work on the phone, it doesn't work, you know. And

07:30.080 --> 07:36.160
increasingly, you know, a majority of readers are often seeing stories on the small screen. And so

07:36.160 --> 07:42.400
I think, you know, from the design standpoint, if not start with it, it has to be paramount or equal

07:42.400 --> 07:48.240
to the desktop. And that's just, you know, now fundamental to kind of the custom web development

07:48.240 --> 07:53.280
our team does. And I think probably every team like ours does. I think, you know, one symptom of

07:53.280 --> 07:58.160
that or one outcome of that is, you know, this won't surprise you, I'm sure is, you know, kind

07:58.160 --> 08:04.320
of a move to more static graphics over interactive graphics, in many cases, because they're oftentimes

08:04.320 --> 08:10.400
just better suited for mobile and sort of the recalculation of the cost benefit analysis of,

08:10.400 --> 08:14.720
you know, how much work you put into something. The increasing weight on mobile as an input,

08:14.720 --> 08:20.320
you know, makes the investment on the interactivity kind of often seem less worth it, you know. And

08:20.320 --> 08:24.000
so I think you're definitely would see a lot more static graphics at the LA Times and pretty much

08:24.000 --> 08:28.880
everywhere across the industry. I think that's probably only going to increase, you know, I think

08:28.880 --> 08:33.680
that trend is going to continue. And I think, you know, you know, really boring, nerdy stuff,

08:33.680 --> 08:39.360
like you got to make sure the page isn't too heavy, you know, and, and you have to think about also

08:39.360 --> 08:45.200
how you can maybe output stories from data in an automated way or things that maybe serve that

08:45.200 --> 08:50.160
mobile audience that maybe wouldn't serve a print or sort of a long reading audience that's putting

08:50.160 --> 08:55.040
more attention in little bits and bytes of information. Like an example I would give is a

08:55.040 --> 09:01.040
few years ago, one of my colleagues developed a bot that automatically writes and publishes

09:01.040 --> 09:06.000
stories about earthquakes within seconds of them happening by converting the sort of pulse of

09:06.000 --> 09:11.760
information that comes out of the government seismographs into a very simple post, which then

09:11.760 --> 09:16.720
is sent to the copy desk automatically who review it before sending it live. And that allows us

09:16.720 --> 09:21.520
almost literally within seconds to have a post with a basic map and a basic summary of the

09:21.520 --> 09:27.040
earthquake ready to ship. And then if it all worked out publish, and that allows us to get that little

09:27.040 --> 09:31.760
bit of information that people more often in a mobile space are trying to find kind of like

09:31.760 --> 09:37.120
immediately, hey, was that a big earthquake here in LA just seconds ago, right? And conceiving of

09:37.120 --> 09:42.160
kind of the audience's needs, from kind of the mobile point of view can lead you to like put an

09:42.160 --> 09:47.280
emphasis on creating more things like that. Yeah, that's really interesting. Everyone says mobile

09:47.280 --> 09:51.120
first, you just said mobile first, are you literally thinking about Oh, we're going to do

09:51.120 --> 09:57.840
this new story on, I don't know, wildfires, is it the development is literally mobile first? Or is

09:57.840 --> 10:04.000
it you know, we have an idea for how we want to tell this story. And now let's make sure that it

10:04.000 --> 10:10.880
works on mobile. So my question is really whether it's thinking initially in a small screen versus

10:10.880 --> 10:15.760
thinking sort of broader and then modifying elements of the story so that they work on a

10:15.760 --> 10:20.560
mobile screen. I mean, in the last couple years, our narrow team, the data desk has kind of evolved

10:20.560 --> 10:26.800
more to projects work, off breaking news work. And so on that particular example you gave hasn't

10:26.800 --> 10:30.800
come up for us. But you know, our graphics desk, which is, you know, just increasing in strength

10:30.800 --> 10:35.760
and does a great job on most of the kind of the breaking news work like that, they definitely are

10:35.760 --> 10:40.880
thinking that way and are taking that approach. When it comes to something more like a long term

10:40.880 --> 10:45.920
project or something that you that you want to have kind of be special. I don't know if we think

10:45.920 --> 10:50.560
mobile first, but we're kind of thinking like what you know, at its bare essence, what is the story

10:50.560 --> 10:56.960
we're trying to tell, right? And then what is the most powerful way to reach, you know, our audience

10:56.960 --> 11:03.120
as we conceive them, you know, with the tools we have. And I think, you know, as part of like that

11:03.120 --> 11:07.200
conception and that idea development process, you have to be thinking about people's phones,

11:07.200 --> 11:11.200
because that is, you know, this gigantic percentage of your audience. And so for me,

11:11.200 --> 11:16.000
when it comes to like the projects work, I think it really begins with what's the story and how do

11:16.000 --> 11:21.520
we best tell it? That's really interesting. You sent out this tweet in December about 30 of the

11:21.520 --> 11:27.040
most viewed pages in 2017 and nine were by apps or designers. Can you talk a little bit more about

11:27.040 --> 11:32.240
that expand about that? You know, what does that I guess really what does it mean to you as someone

11:32.240 --> 11:39.040
who has seen this transition to have about a third of the biggest hits be from your team or from the

11:39.040 --> 11:43.680
development team? To me, it's just a sign that what we're doing is connecting with our audience

11:43.680 --> 11:48.960
and is kind of at the core of what our newsroom is doing to try to change and survive like in the

11:48.960 --> 11:54.560
digital space. One of my sort of maybe secret motivations for writing a post like that is that

11:54.560 --> 12:00.320
one of my kind of pet peeves about particularly about data analysis projects or about sort of the

12:00.320 --> 12:04.160
card investigative work is sometimes there's an idea out there that you know, they don't draw a

12:04.160 --> 12:10.960
large audience, you know, or that they're sort of part of an outdated sort of prize winning process

12:10.960 --> 12:15.920
that happens in newsrooms or something. And I just think that that is at least in our case 100%

12:15.920 --> 12:22.240
untrue and that our team is contributing to and trying to really move the needle with reaching a

12:22.240 --> 12:27.120
digital audience and doing new things. And I think that I think that's almost certainly true,

12:27.120 --> 12:32.080
probably most other teams like ours. And I think it's just an idea that I want to kind of reinforce

12:32.080 --> 12:38.000
and spread because I don't often see it hammered as hard as I think it should because there's

12:38.000 --> 12:42.960
really good reasons to invest in the type of work we do that are, you know, to be frank, you know,

12:42.960 --> 12:48.480
commercial. And I think that's fine with me. You know what I mean? And so that, you know, that's,

12:48.480 --> 12:52.160
that's one reason I think that that's an important story to tell it to is I'm just really proud of

12:52.160 --> 12:56.960
the people who work on our team, because I feel like that's a sign of their hard work paying off.

12:56.960 --> 13:00.000
You know, if you were to look at those nine things, I didn't really elaborate on what they

13:00.000 --> 13:04.800
were, but you would find kind of a broader array of things than you might think of with our team

13:04.800 --> 13:10.320
being called the data desk. They're not all just like data stories. The number one most viewed

13:10.320 --> 13:17.280
thing at the LA Times, at least so far, this year was a series of editorials that our opinion page

13:17.280 --> 13:23.280
put out early in the year about President Donald Trump, and some problems they had with him. And

13:24.080 --> 13:28.400
in an effort to make a bigger splash out of the series of a half dozen editorials,

13:29.360 --> 13:34.720
Andrea Roberson, who's on our data desk team, designed using our data desk custom publishing

13:34.720 --> 13:40.000
tools, a series of pages outside the content management system that really kind of blew these

13:40.000 --> 13:44.400
up and made them look special, you know, and improve their presentation. And you know,

13:44.400 --> 13:48.720
I think obviously, you know, the primary reason that those editorials succeeded is the content

13:48.720 --> 13:52.720
and the writing and the people not on our team who, you know, did all that. But I do think a

13:52.720 --> 13:57.520
significant component of it was the excellent work she did in the design of it. And so that's why I

13:57.520 --> 14:01.120
think, you know, that that would be a case where our team kind of had a hand in in something that

14:01.120 --> 14:07.360
you might not think of as the narrow data work, but was really important. Right. You know, I've

14:07.360 --> 14:12.400
noticed over at the New York Times that they've started adding the developer names to the bylines

14:12.400 --> 14:18.240
of news articles. Have you all started to do that? And if not, is that something that you think,

14:18.240 --> 14:22.720
I think going in line with what you were just talking about of your team being a crucial part

14:22.720 --> 14:27.120
and a central part, is that something that your group is looking to do in 2018?

14:27.120 --> 14:30.720
Oh, totally. Yeah. I mean, it's something we've done for years. Okay, honest. It's something

14:30.720 --> 14:36.480
we've done for I can't even remember, but at least five years or more long time. You know,

14:36.480 --> 14:40.560
there's different ways it gets kind of handled depending on the context. And you could I'm sure

14:40.560 --> 14:45.760
finding consistencies and how it's been applied with zero exceptions, you know, if someone has

14:45.760 --> 14:51.280
done custom web development work that even if it's strictly cosmetic, they receive a production

14:51.280 --> 14:57.120
credit at the bottom of the page. If you know that production work is for like sort of a data

14:57.120 --> 15:03.600
graphic, like say election results, or some sort of standalone visualization, we do traditional

15:03.600 --> 15:09.600
bylines at the top of the page. And then, you know, in cases where it's like a long form narrative

15:09.600 --> 15:16.080
where we haven't done just cosmetic design, but we've also contributed data analysis. There's

15:16.080 --> 15:19.920
sometimes kind of a mix between whether you receive kind of the traditional byline alongside

15:19.920 --> 15:25.040
the writer, which in many cases you do, or, you know, this sort of print tradition of a

15:25.040 --> 15:30.400
contribution line at the bottom of the writing, which is based on some judgment call about, you

15:30.400 --> 15:35.360
know, the significance of the work that's been contributed. And, you know, there's a lot of,

15:35.360 --> 15:39.440
you know, messiness and give and take and when that happens and how, but, you know, to me,

15:39.440 --> 15:43.520
I think it's important that people receive their credit. I, you know, it might be better if we had

15:43.520 --> 15:47.280
kind of stricter rules in a system that everyone was on the same page with. But, you know, these

15:47.280 --> 15:52.240
things are evolving all the time. And to me, it's just something we're consistently trying to sort

15:52.240 --> 15:58.320
out. Yeah. Yeah. I mean, it seems like continued evolution. Oh, yeah. I want to get your thoughts

15:58.320 --> 16:04.720
on what were your favorite stories from 2017? What pieces you all worked on that you thought

16:04.720 --> 16:09.360
were highlights, not necessarily that may not have been the biggest, you know, page views,

16:09.360 --> 16:15.120
but which one sort of struck you as both the editor and as a reader? Sure. I mean, so here

16:15.120 --> 16:20.160
there's a few local or California stories that might not have got all around the world that

16:20.160 --> 16:26.640
our team participated in that I think were real good. Let's see. So our first example might be

16:26.640 --> 16:31.280
Ryan Menezes on our team, who's sort of a actually a statistics person who has become

16:31.280 --> 16:37.600
a journalist on our team partnered with Ivan Penn on our business desk, who's sort of a long time

16:37.600 --> 16:43.120
energy reporter to use data to help Ivan sort of raises ambitions and write a bigger series of

16:43.120 --> 16:51.040
stories about some phenomena. Did I get that right? I never know. Yeah, I'll have to look that

16:51.040 --> 16:57.920
one up. They probably both were wrong. But some stuff. Yeah, so Ivan's an energy reporter on the

16:57.920 --> 17:04.080
beat, right? Right. He knows lots of stuff. But sometimes in just covering things that happen

17:04.960 --> 17:08.720
out there in the world, you can't take a step back and kind of get at the bigger issues you want to

17:08.720 --> 17:12.800
get at with the kind of good enterprise. And that's where data can often help. And in this case,

17:12.880 --> 17:16.720
I think it did where he had sort of been hearing from people and kind of sort of developing the

17:16.720 --> 17:22.480
idea that, Hey, maybe California just has too many power plants just has too much power. And

17:22.480 --> 17:26.320
this is not something that was in any story anywhere or that had really been written about

17:26.320 --> 17:30.560
at all. It was just kind of this thesis that some people on the fringes were kind of putting out

17:30.560 --> 17:35.120
there and he wanted to find out if was true and kind of evaluate that. And so we sort of teamed

17:35.120 --> 17:40.400
Ryan up with them and using a lot of data, some of it publicly available, some of it gathered,

17:41.200 --> 17:48.240
you know, through records requests and other means, were able to like really dig in and go at

17:48.240 --> 17:54.160
that question. And the answer they found was that it's true that California actually has a gigantic

17:54.160 --> 17:59.600
and growing surplus of energy being generated by the power plants out there across the state.

17:59.600 --> 18:05.920
And we continue to build more. And that's actually one of the reasons why price continues to go up.

18:06.480 --> 18:11.440
And so we have this kind of broken supply and demand curve of demand is actually decreasing

18:11.440 --> 18:19.440
due to like energy efficiency, green alternatives, yada, yada, yada. So demand is flat and supply is

18:19.440 --> 18:23.440
increasing, which, you know, if you remember high school economics, that should result in lower

18:23.440 --> 18:28.720
prices, but it's not prices are going up. And the system in some kind of fundamental way is broken.

18:28.720 --> 18:35.600
And they were able to really dig into that through data. We wrote kind of three big kind of stories

18:35.680 --> 18:39.840
through the year, which we tried to bring to life and take make this boring, complicated concept

18:39.840 --> 18:46.640
relatable through illustrations, and sort of a mobile first sort of like slider kind of concept

18:46.640 --> 18:51.200
that was separate from the main stories. And you know, I thought that stuff turned out real good.

18:52.480 --> 18:58.880
It's resulted in a power plant being canceled, which is pretty cool, you know, as far as impact

18:58.880 --> 19:06.240
goes. And was to me just a great case of how statistics and data can help you, you know,

19:06.240 --> 19:12.240
kind of find and tell and prove out stories that otherwise would not be told. And to me,

19:12.240 --> 19:17.520
that's one of the things I get a real kick out of in data journalism. Yeah, absolutely. I mean,

19:17.520 --> 19:21.120
having a huge impact. Were there any others that struck a chord with you this year?

19:22.160 --> 19:25.520
So Ivan's on our business desk, and I am kind of proud this year that we partnered with our

19:25.520 --> 19:29.600
business desk more than we have in past years. And this is kind of, you know, part of that

19:29.600 --> 19:34.400
evolutionary process is you have sort of allies that you gradually develop within the organization.

19:34.400 --> 19:38.800
And then you seek to expand that network, you know, and draw in new people and new sections of the

19:38.800 --> 19:42.880
paper to kind of, you know, the approach we're trying to take. And so this year, we did a bunch

19:42.880 --> 19:46.800
of stories with business that I thought turned out okay, there was the ones with Ivan. And then

19:46.800 --> 19:53.600
there was another set we did with two reporters, Natalie Kitroff and Jeff Mohan, about California's

19:53.600 --> 19:58.720
agricultural industry and how that has really changed in the last couple years, and how some of

19:58.720 --> 20:04.800
sort of the received wisdom and potted history of sort of how that industry works is outdated.

20:04.800 --> 20:09.280
And a lot of things are changing. So like, for instance, due to increased border enforcement,

20:09.280 --> 20:14.560
there's actually tons and tons of people coming in through legal visa programs for temporary farm

20:14.560 --> 20:19.760
